# PART 2 — Package Transitive Dependency Mapping

## 2.1 Purpose

Map every package dependency edge — direct and transitive — with two
query modes:
1. **Full graph** — the entire resolved tree.
2. **Target lookup** — every path from a manifest-level dependency down to
   a given package, with depth and intermediates.

This Part does not re-implement any package manager's resolution
algorithm. It orchestrates each ecosystem's own resolver.

## 2.2 Scope & Non-Goals

**In scope:** npm/yarn/pnpm, pip/poetry/pipenv, Maven/Gradle, NuGet,
Cargo, Go modules.

**Not covered — stated plainly, not silently degraded to "Not detected":**
PHP (Composer), Ruby (Bundler), Swift (SPM/CocoaPods), Dart (pub), Elixir
(Mix), C/C++ (no dominant ecosystem — Conan/vcpkg/system packages). On a
repo in one of these this Part produces nothing useful. Adding one is
mechanical — a Fidelity Ledger row plus an Appendix entry — not a
redesign.

**Out of scope by design:** vulnerability severity scoring (structure
only — see Part 4 for .NET remediation); license extraction (§2.11).

## 2.3 Fidelity Ledger

**Single authority per ecosystem.** Manifests are never hand-parsed to
*infer* a graph: manifests express ranges, not resolution — only the
resolver (or the lockfile it produced) knows the resolved answer.

| Ecosystem | Authority | Rejected alternative | Why rejected |
|---|---|---|---|
| npm | `npm ls --all --json`, `npm explain` | Hand-parsing `package.json` ranges | Ranges ≠ resolved versions; misses hoisting/dedup |
| yarn | `yarn why`, `yarn list --json` | Re-deriving from `package.json` | Dedup/hoisting logic is undocumented and version-specific |
| pnpm | `pnpm why`, `pnpm list --depth Infinity --json` | Walking `node_modules/.pnpm` symlinks | Content-addressed store layout changes across versions |
| pip | `pipdeptree --json-tree` | Chaining `pip show` manually | `pip show` gives one hop only |
| poetry | `poetry show --tree` | pip-style resolution | Poetry has its own resolver; pip's view of the same venv can disagree |
| Maven | `mvn dependency:tree -Dverbose` | Reading `<dependencies>` in `pom.xml` | Misses transitive mediation and conflict-omitted versions entirely |
| Gradle | `./gradlew <module>:dependencies` | Reading `build.gradle` | Conflict resolution (highest-wins) is not visible in source |
| NuGet | `dotnet list package --include-transitive` | Parsing `.csproj`/`packages.lock.json` by hand | Central Package Management and floating versions break static parsing |
| Cargo | `cargo tree`, `cargo tree -i` | `Cargo.lock` manual parse | `cargo tree` already resolves feature unification; native reverse mode exists |
| Go | `go mod graph`, `go mod why -m` | Parsing `go.sum` | `go.sum` is a checksum ledger, not a dependency graph |

`pipdeptree` is an accelerator: installed if missing, never assumed. If a
resolver binary is absent, report **"Not run — resolver unavailable:
[tool] not found on PATH"**, never approximate by manifest-parsing.

## 2.4 Phase 0 — Certification Harness (mandatory gate)

Report PASS/FAIL with evidence. A FAIL halts that ecosystem only; others
proceed independently.

1. **Manifest present** — cite the path.
2. **Lockfile present**, or explicit justification. Without one, resolution
   is non-deterministic — a fidelity caveat, not a blocker.
3. **Resolver binary on PATH**, version captured.
4. **Resolvable without network mutation** — prefer lockfile-only flags
   (`npm ls --package-lock-only`, `pnpm list --offline`). If a live
   install is required to materialize a tree (Maven needs artifacts
   present), state it and require the operator to run the install first
   rather than silently triggering a network install.
5. **Dry-run parse check** — run the resolver against one known-good
   package and confirm output parses into the expected schema.

**Gate:** proceed if 1–3 and 5 pass. Check 4 downgrades confidence.

## 2.5 Phase 1 — Ecosystem Detection & Inventory

Monorepo-aware (one level of workspace detection). Record per match:
ecosystem, manifest path, lockfile path (or "Not detected"), and
workspace/module name — npm/yarn/pnpm workspaces, Maven multi-module
`<modules>`, Cargo workspace members, Go module path.

| Ecosystem | Manifest | Lockfile | Workspace |
|---|---|---|---|
| npm | `packages/web/package.json` | `package-lock.json` (root) | web |
| poetry | `services/api/pyproject.toml` | `poetry.lock` | api |

Zero manifests → **"Not detected — no supported ecosystem manifest found
in scanned paths: [list]"**. Stop.

## 2.6 Phase 2 — Graph Extraction

```json
{
  "ecosystem": "npm",
  "resolver": "npm@10.8.2",
  "source": "npm ls --all --json",
  "resolved_at": "2026-07-28T00:00:00Z",
  "edges": [
    { "from": "my-app@1.0.0", "to": "express@4.19.2",
      "direct": true, "scope": "runtime" },
    { "from": "express@4.19.2", "to": "body-parser@1.20.2",
      "direct": false, "scope": "runtime" }
  ]
}
```

Store raw resolver output alongside the normalized JSON so any edge can be
audited back to source. No edge is inferred or interpolated.

## 2.7 Phase 3 — Classification & Path Reconstruction

- **Classification** — `direct` (in the manifest) vs `transitive`.
- **Scope** — `runtime` | `dev` | `test` | `build`. npm `devDependencies`,
  Maven `test` scope, Cargo `dev-dependencies` are **never merged into one
  undifferentiated list**; a dev-only vulnerability has a different risk
  profile from a runtime one.
- **Depth** — shortest-path hops from a direct dependency.
- **All resolution paths** — a package reachable via multiple direct
  dependencies (diamond) gets every distinct path enumerated, not just the
  shortest. Impact analysis needs the full set.
- **Multiple resolved versions** — if the same package resolves to several
  versions in one tree (common in npm without full dedup), each version is
  listed separately with its own path set and its own pulling parent.

## 2.8 Phase 4 — Target Lookup Mode

1. Locate the target (all matching versions).
2. Walk reverse edges to every direct dependency reaching it.
3. Report each path in full:

```
express@4.19.2 (direct, package.json:14, scope=runtime)
  └─ body-parser@1.20.2
       └─ qs@6.11.0  ← TARGET (depth 3)
```

4. Absent → **"Not detected — <package> not present in resolved graph for
   <ecosystem>/<workspace> as of <resolver> run at <timestamp>"**.
5. **Prefer the native reverse command** over reimplementing the backward
   walk: `npm explain`, `yarn why`, `pnpm why`,
   `pipdeptree --reverse --packages`, `cargo tree -i`, `go mod why -m`,
   `dotnet nuget why`. Cross-check native output against the graph walk;
   on disagreement report the discrepancy rather than silently preferring
   one.

## 2.9 Phase 5 — Conflict, Risk & Duplication Detection

Evidence-only flags, each citing packages/versions:

- **Multiple resolved versions** of one package — all versions, with which
  direct dependency pulled each.
- **Excessive depth** — chains beyond a configurable threshold (default 6).
- **Unpinned transitive ranges** in the lockfile. Report **"Not
  applicable"** for ecosystems whose lockfiles always pin exact versions
  (`Cargo.lock`, `poetry.lock`).
- **Single-path fragility** — a widely-used transitive package reachable
  via only one direct dependency; removing it silently drops the package.
- **Scope leakage** — a package in both `dev` and `runtime` scope at
  different versions.

### Weighted scorecard (A–F) per workspace

| Factor | Weight | Evidence required |
|---|---|---|
| Version duplication | 25% | count + package list |
| Max depth | 20% | deepest chain(s), cited |
| Unpinned transitive count | 25% | count, or "Not applicable" |
| Single-path fragility | 30% | count + package list |

Grade bands: A ≥90, B ≥80, C ≥70, D ≥60, F <60.

**Confidence annotation** on every score: `High` (lockfile present, Phase
0 check 4 clean), `Medium` (lockfile present, live resolution required),
`Low` (no lockfile — resolution non-deterministic, so counts may not
reproduce).

## 2.10 Phase 6 — Outputs

1. **`dependency-intelligence-report.md`** — inventory, scorecard with
   confidence, target-lookup results, flagged risks; every claim citing a
   manifest/lockfile path and the exact resolver command run.
2. **`dependency-graph.mmd`** — Mermaid, paginated for large trees:

```mermaid
graph TD
  A[my-app] --> B[express@4.19.2]
  B --> C[body-parser@1.20.2]
  C --> D[qs@6.11.0]
```

3. **`dependency-graph.json`** — normalized edge list so downstream
   consumers need not re-run resolution.

## 2.11 Part 2 Limitations

- **No CVE or vulnerability data.** Structure only. Do not present this
  output as a security scan.
- **No license data.** Treat licensing questions as "Not detected".
- **Six ecosystems only** (§2.2).
- **Optional peerDependencies / extras / features** appear only if
  satisfied at resolution time; reported per-ecosystem based on what the
  authority tool surfaces.
- **Private/internal registries**: if the resolver cannot reach one and
  Phase 0 check 4 fails, that subtree is marked low-confidence rather than
  omitted. A silent registry failure looks identical to a clean tree —
  which is why check 4 is reported, not skipped.
- **No lockfile means no reproducibility.** Two runs can legitimately
  differ; the scorecard confidence annotation carries this.

