# PART 4 — .NET / NuGet OSS Remediation

## 4.1 Purpose

Take a .NET solution from "unknown OSS risk" to "remediated and verified,"
with evidence at every step. Four categories: known vulnerabilities,
deprecated/unlisted packages, outdated-but-not-vulnerable packages, and
**transitive-only vulnerabilities** — the category that matters most,
because the package appears in no `.csproj` and a source-only review will
never find it.

## 4.2 Fidelity Ledger

| Concern | Authority | Rejected alternative | Why rejected |
|---|---|---|---|
| Vulnerability data | NuGetAudit during `dotnet restore` (GitHub Advisory Database via nuget.org) | Third-party SCA as primary | Adds a second source of truth; use as corroboration, not authority |
| Resolved package set | `dotnet list package --include-transitive` | Parsing `.csproj`/`packages.lock.json` by hand | CPM, floating versions and transitive resolution break static parsing |
| Root-cause tracing | `dotnet nuget why <pkg>` | Manual tree reading | Native reverse lookup; equivalent to `npm explain` / `cargo tree -i` |
| Fix verification | Re-run of the detection command | Assuming the edit worked | An unverified fix is not a fix |

## 4.3 Phase 0 — Certification Harness (mandatory gate)

1. **SDK version captured** — `dotnet --version`. Audit behavior differs
   materially by version (§4.4).
2. **Solution restores cleanly.** A failing restore means audit data is
   incomplete, not that the solution is clean.
3. **Audit source reachable.** Confirm the configured package/audit source
   returns vulnerability data. **The single most important check in this
   Part.** An enterprise on a curated private feed with no public feed
   access gets *no vulnerability data at all*, and the output is identical
   to a clean solution. If unreachable, report **"Not run — audit source
   unreachable: [source]. Absence of findings is NOT evidence of absence
   of vulnerabilities."** and stop.
4. **Baseline captured** — full `dotnet list package --include-transitive`
   output stored before any modification.
5. **Build and tests pass pre-change** — a red baseline makes post-fix
   breakage unattributable.

**Gate: all five.** Check 3 failing halts the run entirely.

## 4.4 Phase 1 — Configure Audit Scope

`NuGetAuditMode` controls scope: `direct` (project references only) or
`all` (direct **and** transitive).

**The default changed by version.** Targeting .NET 10 or higher the
default is `all`; below that it remains `direct`. Do not rely on the
default — set it explicitly in `Directory.Build.props`:

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
  <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
  <NuGetAuditLevel>moderate</NuGetAuditLevel>
</PropertyGroup>
```

If the team uses `<TreatWarningsAsErrors>` and needs findings not to break
the build immediately, scope the exemption narrowly rather than disabling
audit:

```xml
<WarningsNotAsErrors>NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;$(WarningsNotAsErrors)</WarningsNotAsErrors>
```

Record the choice — a suppressed-warning build and a clean build are not
the same result.

## 4.5 Phase 2 — Detection

```
dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive
dotnet list package --deprecated
dotnet list package --outdated --include-transitive
```

`--include-transitive` is **not** the default. Omitting it hides exactly
the category this Part exists for.

Per finding record: package id, requested vs resolved version,
direct/transitive, severity, advisory URL, affected projects. No finding
is reported without its advisory URL.

## 4.6 Phase 3 — Root-Cause Tracing

```
dotnet nuget why <transitive-package-id>
```

Identifies the top-level package pulling in the vulnerable dependency.
Record the full chain. **Remediating the parent is preferred over
overriding the child** — §4.7.

## 4.7 Phase 4 — Remediation, in order of preference

**4.7a Direct package.** `dotnet add package <id> --version <fixed>`

**4.7b Transitive — upgrade the parent first.** If a newer parent version
already depends on a patched version, this is the cleanest fix: no
override, no untested combination. Try before 4.7c.

**4.7c Transitive — explicit direct reference (override).** NuGet uses
nearest-wins, so a direct `PackageReference` to the patched version forces
it up the tree.

```xml
<PackageReference Include="System.Text.Json" Version="8.0.5" />
```

**Honest risk:** brittle at scale. You now reference a package you don't
use, pinning a version the parent was never tested against. Can produce
runtime failures that compile cleanly. Document each override with a
reason and a removal condition ("remove when parent ships a version
depending on ≥8.0.5").

**4.7d Central Package Management.** Bump centrally in
`Directory.Packages.props`; use `VersionOverride` on the single project
needing an exception rather than fragmenting the central version.

**4.7e Accepted-risk suppression — last resort, documented.**
```xml
<NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx" />
```
**Do not fully trust this.** There is a known inconsistency where
`dotnet list package --vulnerable` does not always honor suppression the
same way `restore` does. Re-verify after suppressing. Every suppression
needs an owner, rationale, and review date.

## 4.8 Phase 5 — Verification Gate (mandatory, blocks completion)

1. **Advisory cleared** — re-run detection; confirm the specific advisory
   is absent. Diff against the Phase 0 baseline.
2. **No new findings introduced** — the upgrade may have pulled in new
   transitive packages with their own advisories. Compare full sets.
3. **Build succeeds.**
4. **Tests pass** at or above the Phase 0 baseline rate.

Any failure → revert and report as **unremediated with cause**, rather
than leaving a partially applied fix. Never report a fix complete on the
basis of the edit having been made.

## 4.9 Outputs

`remediation-plan.json`:
```json
{
  "findings": [
    { "package": "System.Text.Json", "resolved": "8.0.3",
      "fixed_version": "8.0.5", "transitive": true,
      "root_cause": "Some.Parent.Package@2.1.0",
      "advisory": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-...",
      "action": "4.7c-override", "verified": true }
  ],
  "suppressions": [],
  "audit_source_reachable": true
}
```

## 4.10 Part 4 Limitations

- **Private feed blindness.** Without reachable advisory data there are no
  findings — indistinguishable from a clean solution. §4.3 check 3 is the
  only thing preventing a false all-clear.
- **False positives from runtime hoisting.** Packages hoisted to a newer,
  non-vulnerable version by the shared runtime can still be reported
  vulnerable. Verify the actually-loaded assembly version before
  overriding a framework-provided package.
- **Advisory lag.** A clean result means no *known, published* advisory as
  of this run. Re-run on a schedule, not once.
- **No reachability analysis.** This reports that a vulnerable package is
  present, not that the vulnerable code path is callable. A finding is not
  automatically an exploitable risk; severity is a prioritization input,
  not a verdict.
- **Overrides create untested combinations** (§4.7c). Compiling is not
  evidence of runtime compatibility.

